There is no existing directory at "/var/www/html/storage/logs" and it could not be created: Permission denied

This is a classic permissions error that happens when the Laravel application running inside the Docker container tries to write to its log file (laravel.log), but the web server user (usually www-data) doesn't have ownership or write access to the storage folder.

Since your container does not use volume mounts (it's an isolated box), the permissions must be fixed directly inside the container's environment.

The Fix: Create and Fix Permissions Inside Docker
Run these three commands in your Contabo terminal to manually create the directory and grant the correct permissions to the web server:

Bash
# 1. Force create the logs directory inside the container
docker exec -it --user root vendorpulse-app-1 mkdir -p /var/www/html/storage/logs

# 2. Change the ownership of the entire storage folder to the web server user (www-data)
docker exec -it --user root vendorpulse-app-1 chown -R www-data:www-data /var/www/html/storage

# 3. Grant proper read/write permissions to the storage directory
docker exec -it --user root vendorpulse-app-1 chmod -R 775 /var/www/html/storage
Step 2: Clear the Application Cache
Once the permissions are fixed, force Laravel to clear its configuration cache so it can attempt to boot cleanly again:

Bash
docker exec -it vendorpulse-app-1 php artisan optimize:clear
Why did this happen?
When you ran the docker cp commands or executed updates earlier as the root user, the storage/ directory structure likely ended up owned by root. When a web visitor hits your Next.js frontend, the frontend talks to the Laravel API, which runs under the www-data user. Because www-data cannot write to a root-owned folder, the application crashes before it can even load the dashboard.

Refresh your dashboard page now—the permission blockage is gone!